27: ## 1. Product Philosophy & Positioning
28:
29: The product must **not** be thought of, built, marketed, or discussed internally as a "CRM." A CRM is a passive system of record — it stores data and waits for a human to act on it. This product is fundamentally different in kind.
30:
31: **Official positioning: Admission Operations Engine.**
32:
33: The defining idea is:
34:
35: > Every lead becomes an autonomous workflow, managed continuously by the system, until it is either converted into a student or explicitly marked as closed.
36:
37: This is the single sentence that should govern every design decision from here forward. If a proposed feature does not fit inside "manage the lead's workflow automatically," it does not belong in the core product — it belongs in a peripheral tool.
38:
39: ### 1.1 Why "CRM" is the wrong mental model
40:
41: A CRM:
42: - Stores lead records.
43: - Requires a human to remember to follow up.
44: - Requires a human to decide what to send.
45: * Requires a human to decide when to escalate.
46: - Calculates analytics after the fact, in batch.
47:
48: An Admission Operations Engine:
49: - Treats every lead as a live, running process (a "workflow instance").
50: - Decides for itself when to follow up, what to send, and when to escalate.
51: - Updates analytics in real time, as a side-effect of the workflow itself.
52: - Reduces the admin's job to counseling, decision-making, and admissions — not remembering, chasing, or repeating information.
53:
54: ### 1.2 What "no intelligence required" means
55:
56: A recurring theme across the whole architecture: most of the system does **not** need AI/LLM intelligence to function. Template selection, follow-up timing, escalation rules, and reminders are all **deterministic, rule-based automation**. AI/LLM capability, if used at all, is reserved for a narrow layer (e.g., the Recommendation Engine's "next best action" reasoning) — not for generating conversational replies. This keeps behavior predictable, auditable, and safe for a business-critical admissions process.
57:
58: ### 1.3 Product goal (measure of success)
59:
60: The success of the PERC Admission Operations Engine is **not** measured by how intelligently it chats. It is measured by:
61: - How effectively it reduces manual work.
62: - How well it standardizes operations.
63: - How reliably it prevents lead leakage (no enquiry ever silently dies).
64: - How well it keeps every stakeholder informed.
65: - How much it maximizes enquiry-to-admission conversion through disciplined workflow automation.
66:
67: ---
68:
69: ## 2. Core Mental Model
70:
71: ### 2.1 Every Lead is a Workflow Instance
72:
73: Instead of storing only a flat "Lead" record, the system stores a composite object per lead:
74:
75: ```
76: Lead
77:  ├── Workflow        (current state, transition history, active timers)
78:  ├── Conversation     (all inbound/outbound messages across channels)
79:  ├── Tasks            (pending human actions: call, meeting, review)
80:  ├── Calendar         (scheduled calls/meetings tied to this lead)
81:  ├── Timeline         (chronological event log — the "memory" of the lead)
82:  └── Analytics        (real-time computed metrics tied to this lead and rolled up globally)
83: ```
84:
85: Every one of these six sub-objects is written to and read from continuously by the various engines described in Section 8. None of them is optional — a "Lead" without a Workflow is just a contact; a "Lead" without a Timeline cannot be automated safely because no engine would know what has already happened.
86:
87: ### 2.2 It Is a State Machine, Not a Conversation
88:
89: The moment an enquiry is captured, it stops being "a chat" and becomes "a running process." The system does not wait passively for messages — it actively drives the lead forward (or sideways into recovery, or out into closure) based on state, elapsed time, and lead behavior.
90:
91: ### 2.3 "The system should schedule itself"
92:
93: This is a foundational requirement, not a nice-to-have. Nothing in the system should depend on an admin remembering to do something later. Every time-based action — a follow-up, a reminder, an escalation, a report — must be represented as a **scheduled job**, created automatically the moment the triggering event happens.
94:
95: ---
96:
97: ## 3. Lead Lifecycle
98:
99: ### 3.1 Canonical Lifecycle Diagram
100:
101: ```
102: New Enquiry
103:    ↓
104: Lead Created
105:    ↓
106: Information Shared
107:    ↓
108: Waiting
109:    ↓
110: Lead Replied?
111:    ├── YES → Continue Journey
112:    └── NO  → Follow-up
113:                  ↓
114:             Interested?
115:                  ├── YES → Schedule Call
116:                  │            ↓
117:                  │       Call Completed?
118:                  │            ├── YES → Interested?
119:                  │            │            ├── YES → Admission
120:                  │            │            │            ↓
121:                  │            │            │       Student Created
122:                  │            │            │            ↓
123:                  │            │            │        Completed
124:                  │            │            └── NO  → Recovery Flow → Closed
125:                  │            └── NO  → Recovery Flow → Closed
126:                  └── NO  → Recovery Flow → Closed
127: ```
128:
129: ### 3.2 Detailed Lifecycle / Pipeline Stages
130:
131: The full enumerated pipeline stage list (used for status fields, dashboard filters, and analytics buckets):
132:
133: 1. **New** — enquiry just captured, nothing sent yet.
134: 2. **Information Shared** — an automated response/template/document has been sent.
135: 3. **Waiting for Response** — system is in a monitoring period, timers active.
136: 4. **Interested** — lead has signaled positive intent (explicit reply, request for call, etc.).
137: 5. **Call Scheduled** — a call has been booked with an admin/counselor.
138: 6. **Meeting Completed** — the scheduled call/meeting has occurred.
139: 7. **Demo Scheduled** — a demo class has been booked (education-specific step).
140: 8. **Admission Pending** — lead has agreed to join; paperwork/payment in progress.
141: 9. **Admitted** — student record created; admission workflow complete.
142: 10. **Inactive** — no meaningful activity for a defined period; not yet lost.
143: 11. **Recovery** — a re-engagement workflow has been triggered for a stalled/inactive/uninterested lead.
144: 12. **Lost** — lead explicitly declined or recovery attempts exhausted.
145: 13. **Closed** — workflow terminated (via Admitted, Lost, or manual admin closure).
146:
147: ### 3.3 Terminal States
148:
149: A lead's workflow is only considered finished when it reaches one of these four terminal states:
150: - Admission Completed
151: - Lead Closed
152: - Lead Marked Lost
153: - Lead Archived
154:
155: Until one of these is reached, the lead is an **active workflow** and is continuously evaluated by the Automation Orchestrator (Section 8.11).
156:
