247:
248: ## 8. The 11 Core Engines — Detailed Specification
249:
250: The entire platform is decomposed into 11 engines. Every new feature request should be mapped to exactly one (or a coordination of a few) of these engines rather than becoming an isolated, one-off module. This is what keeps the system easy to design, build, test, and scale as it grows.
251:
252: ### 8.1 Engine 1 — Lead Capture Engine
253:
254: **Purpose:** Capture every enquiry, regardless of source, and turn it into a fully-formed, workflow-ready Lead record.
255:
256: **Inputs:** Website, WhatsApp, Instagram, Facebook, Google Business, Phone, Walk-in, Email, Referral (see Section 5 for full list).
257:
258: **Responsibilities:**
259: - Capture enquiry
260: - Identify source
261: - Validate information
262: - Generate Lead ID
263: - Save metadata
264: - Store timestamp
265: - Store contact details
266: - Store initial enquiry content
267: - Detect enquiry category (e.g., fee enquiry, course enquiry, general enquiry)
268: - Create Lead record
269: - Create Timeline (initialize the event log)
270: - Initialize Workflow (put the lead into its first state)
271: - Trigger downstream events
272:
273: **Outputs:**
274: - Lead Created (event)
275: - Timeline Created
276: - Workflow Created
277: - Admin Notification
278: - Dashboard Updated
279:
280: ### 8.2 Engine 2 — Response Template Engine
281:
282: **Purpose:** Provide the correct information back to the lead — **without AI**. This is a deterministic template/content-selection engine, not a conversational generator.
283:
284: **Responsibilities:**
285: - Select the correct response template based on detected enquiry category
286: - Attach brochures
287: - Attach fee documents
288: - Attach course documents
289: - Attach location details
290: - Attach admission documents
291: - Send acknowledgement message
292: - Maintain template versions (so old vs. new brochures/fees don't get mixed up)
293: - Support multilingual templates
294:
295: **Dependencies:** Message Template Library, Document Repository, Course Repository (see Section 10).
296:
297: **Example category → content mapping:**
298:
299: | Enquiry Category | Content Sent |
300: |---|---|
301: | Fee enquiry | Fee PDF, Brochure, Fee Structure, Acknowledgement |
302: | Location enquiry | Google Maps link, Address, Phone, Timings |
303: | General enquiry | Institution Brochure, Courses, Faculty, Contact Number |
304:
305: No intelligence is required for this mapping — it is a lookup table, not a reasoning task.
306:
307: ### 8.3 Engine 3 — Workflow Engine ⭐
308:
309: **Purpose:** This is the heart of the platform. Every lead owns exactly one active workflow, and this engine is what advances that workflow through its states.
310:
311: **Responsibilities:**
312: - Lead state management
313: - Workflow state management
314: - Decision logic (what should happen next, given the current state and latest event)
315: - Workflow progression (move the lead forward)
316: - Workflow completion (reach a terminal state)
317: - Workflow cancellation (stop a workflow, e.g., lead unsubscribes)
318: - Workflow recovery (restart/re-engage a stalled workflow)
319:
320: **States (canonical list — matches Section 3.2):** New, Information Shared, Waiting, Interested, Call Scheduled, Meeting Scheduled, Demo, Admission Pending, Admitted, Inactive, Recovery, Closed.
321:
322: **Example workflow instance (illustrative):**
323: ```
324: Workflow
325:    ↓
326: Wait 2 Hours
327:    ↓
328: Check Reply
329:    ↓
330: No Reply?
331:    ↓
332: Send Follow-up
333:    ↓
334: Wait 24 Hours
335:    ↓
336: Still No Reply?
337:    ↓
338: Notify Admin
339:    ↓
340: Recovery
341: ```
342: The system is, at any given moment, maintaining hundreds (eventually thousands) of these workflow instances simultaneously — this is the scaling challenge the rest of the architecture (Section 9) is designed to solve.
343:
344: ### 8.4 Engine 4 — Scheduler Engine
345:
346: **Purpose:** Execute future actions automatically. "The system should schedule itself" (Section 2.3) is implemented here. This is not limited to meeting scheduling — it schedules **any** future action.
347:
348: **Responsibilities:**
349: - Schedule follow-ups
350: - Schedule calls
351: - Schedule meetings
352: - Schedule reminders
353: - Schedule escalations
354: - Schedule reports
355: - Cancel scheduled jobs
356: - Reschedule tasks
357: - Retry failed jobs
358: - Maintain the job queue
359:
360: **Examples:**
361: ```
362: Lead Created → Scheduler → Wait 2 Hours → Check Reply
363: Call Completed → Scheduler → Tomorrow → Feedback Message
364: Demo Completed → Scheduler → 6 PM → Follow-up
365: ```
366: Everything that happens later is represented as a scheduled job — nothing is left to human memory.
367:
368: ### 8.5 Engine 5 — Conversation Timeline Engine
369:
370: **Purpose:** Maintain the complete, chronological history of every interaction with a lead. This is explicitly **not AI** — it is simply structured memory.
371:
372: **Responsibilities:**
373: - Log every message
374: - Log every document shared
375: - Log every call
376: - Log every meeting
377: - Log workflow transitions
378: - Log notifications
379: - Log admin actions
380: - Generate the timeline view
381: - Support auditing
382:
383: **Example timeline for a lead ("Rahul"):**
384: ```
385: 10:05 — Asked Fee
386: 10:06 — Fee PDF Sent
387: 10:07 — Brochure Sent
388: 10:30 — Asked About Hostel
389: 10:31 — Hostel PDF Sent
390: 11:00 — Requested Call
391: ```
392: Every future automation decision reads this timeline before acting — it is the single source of truth for "what has already happened," and it is what allows the Follow-up Engine (8.6) to avoid duplicate or irrelevant actions.
393:
394: ### 8.6 Engine 6 — Follow-up Engine
395:
396: **Purpose:** Ensure that no lead is ever silently forgotten — but do so intelligently, not blindly.
397:
398: **Critical design correction (from source discussion):** Do **not** implement follow-up logic as a naive timer:
399: ```
400: After 2 hours → Send Follow-up   ✗ (wrong — naive)
401: ```
402: Instead, implement it as an **evaluation** step:
403: ```
404: After 2 hours → Evaluate Situation
405:    → Has replied?      YES → Cancel Follow-up
406:                         NO  → Send Follow-up #1
407:    → Has admin already called?   YES → Cancel
408:    → Lead already admitted?      YES → Cancel Everything
409: ```
410: **Responsibilities:**
411: - Monitor inactivity
412: - Evaluate lead status before every scheduled action fires (re-check relevance)
413: - Select the correct follow-up template for the current stage
414: - Send follow-up
415: - Cancel follow-ups that are no longer relevant
416: - Create recovery workflows for stalled/uninterested leads
417: - Stop all workflows immediately after admission
418: - Track follow-up effectiveness (for the Analytics Engine)
419:
420: **Design principle:** every scheduled action must first check whether it is still relevant before executing. This single rule prevents the single most common failure mode of naive automation systems: sending a "please reply" follow-up to someone who already replied, was already called, or already enrolled.
421:
422: ### 8.7 Engine 7 — Call & Meeting Coordination Engine ⭐⭐⭐
423:
424: **Purpose:** Coordinate all human-to-human communication (calls, meetings, demos) between leads and admins/counselors. This is called out as something most CRMs handle poorly, and is therefore a key differentiator.
425:
426: **Responsibilities:**
427: - Meeting scheduling
428: - Call scheduling
429: - Availability management (checking admin calendars)
430: - Rescheduling
431: - Calendar integration
432: - Reminder generation (to both lead and admin)
433: - Missed call handling
434: - Meeting completion tracking
435: - Feedback collection
436:
437: **Happy path example:**
438: ```
439: Lead: "I want to talk."
440:    ↓
441: System checks admin availability
